PR01|Cash-flow and payoff engine|M01 M21 M22 M24 M25 B03|Reconstruct arbitrary signed vanilla legs including fees and settlement obligations.|Define typed contracts and signed legs; compute per-unit then multiplier cash; sum stock and option cash flows; add assignment branch and manual comparison.|Executable functions, accessible payoff table, five independently worked cases and unit tests.|100/105 call spread purchased for 2 pays 0/0/5 at spot 90/100/110; net -2/-2/3 per unit before fees. Long/short legs must cancel exactly absent costs.|Wrong put sign; double premium; adjusted multiplier; expired contract; one-leg assignment.|Terminal payoff is not margin, interim risk or guaranteed fill; calendars require dated valuation.|10,20
PR02|BSM pricing library|M29 M30 M13 B03|Implement a conditional European pricing reference with explicit units and edge handling.|Write scalar normal CDF with documented implementation; handle T=0 and sigma=0; add carry q; compare put-call parity and tree convergence; record model assumptions.|Versioned library, derivation note, input contract and tolerance report.|S=K=100, r=.05,q=0,sigma=.2,T=1: C =10.45058357,P =5.57352602; C-P =4.87705755. Absolute tolerance 1e-7 for benchmark.|Negative T/sigma; percent-as-decimal; cancellation deep ITM; dividend mismatch.|Not American pricing or an actual expected return; discrete dividends need a separate model.|15,30
PR03|Greeks visualizer and convention audit|PR02 M32 M33|Connect derivative definitions with finite shocks and portfolio cash exposures.|Define analytic delta/gamma/vega/rho/theta; select central-difference steps over a convergence grid; add vanna/vomma/charm and full revaluation; label axes and textual equivalents.|Plots plus tabular alternatives, Greek dictionary, analytic/numerical comparisons and residual surface.|At PR02 benchmark delta .63683065,gamma .01876202,vega 37.52403469 per unit vol,theta -6.41402755 per year at fixed expiry; vega per point .37524035.|Time-sign inversion; raw-vega mixing; unstable finite difference; lambda near zero value.|Local sensitivities degrade near expiry, jumps and large shocks; surface convention is a scenario assumption.|12,25
PR04|Historical realized-volatility analysis|M11 M12 M35 B03 M54|Compare estimators against a declared forecast target and sampling scheme.|Generate seeded return paths and optional entitled closes; document adjustments/calendar; compute close-to-close, range and intraday estimators where inputs exist; separate overnight; bootstrap in blocks; compare forecasts.|Data dictionary, estimator notebook, uncertainty bands and leakage audit.|Daily sample SD .01 annualized sqrt 252 gives .15874508; constant log returns have zero demeaned sample variance when n >1. Zero-mean RV need not be zero.|Insufficient samples; microstructure noise; using high-low without valid OHLC; revised series.|Synthetic behavior validates code, not empirical forecast edge; annualization is convention.|12,25
PR05|Implied-volatility solver|PR02 M31|Invert only prices inside model-consistent bounds and expose ill conditioning.|Calculate bounds; bracket a monotone European price; use safeguarded root; report price residual and vega; reject out-of-bounds and expired ambiguous quotes; test bid/ask IV interval.|Solver, diagnostic statuses and edge-case test report.|PR02 call price recovers .2 within 1e-7; price above discounted spot rejected; zero-vega regions flagged despite small price residual.|Unbracketed Newton; negative vol; false precision near expiry; American quote fed to European engine.|IV is model/convention dependent, not a forecast; some valid boundary prices imply zero or infinite limiting IV.|10,20
PR06|Arbitrage-aware volatility surface|PR05 M37 M54|Construct a price/surface representation with explicit scope of arbitrage checks.|Generate European synthetic strikes/expiries; synchronized forwards and discounts; filter bad quotes; fit constraints; check strike bounds, monotonicity, convexity and calendar at fixed forward moneyness; stress tails and missing quotes; compare American quote treatment.|Clean chain, exclusion log, fit residuals within bid/ask where possible, arbitrage audit and extrapolation policy.|Flat 20% surface yields w=.04T; calls decrease and are convex in strike. Positive variance alone must not pass. Finite-grid pass is not a global proof.|Crossed/stale quotes; negative forward; nonconvex interpolation; mismatched dividends; unexamined American quotes.|No guarantee outside the grid without analytic conditions; calibration nonuniqueness and quote uncertainty remain.|25,50
PR07|Discrete delta-hedging simulator|PR02 PR03 M34 M47|Reconcile hedge cash and residual error under costs, jumps and funding.|Simulate seeded GBM and jump stress; initialize option and hedge cash; accrue funding; rebalance at fixed times or bands; settle; compare frequencies and turnover; attribute P&L.|Path ledger, replication error distribution, cost sensitivity and independent hand path.|With option and hedge positions zero total P&L zero; self-financing cash changes offset share-trade cash before fees. Error generally narrows with finer hedging in ideal continuous model, not guaranteed pathwise or net of costs.|Hindsight delta; omitted initial premium; wrong hedge sign; missing interest; perfect jump hedge.|Model-world replication is not empirical volatility arbitrage; parameter and dividend uncertainty matter.|20,40
PR08|Lifecycle strategy backtest|PR01 M54 M55 M56|Test a prespecified strategy under executable costs, corporate actions and capital constraints.|Freeze hypothesis and search log; chronological train/validation/holdout; create timestamped universe; delay orders; model package/non-fill/partial-fill; settle assignment; enforce cash/margin; compare baseline and cost stress.|Immutable configuration, event ledger, trial log, hand-reconciled sample, holdout report and reject/adopt memo.|A round trip across 2.00/2.20 costs .20 per unit before fees; net must fall as independent fees rise with fixed trades. Same input yields same ledger.|Look-ahead; survivorship; stale IV; unlimited funding; optimistic midpoint fills; repeated holdout access.|Simulation cannot prove future profit; absent licensed chains use synthetic and make no claim of historical edge.|30,60
